## 12. END-TO-END ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA

The work is not complete merely because code files were generated.

Verify the following end-to-end workflows:

A. Registration:
A new user registers with a unique username, valid email, and compliant password. The verification email is delivered through the configured provider. The OTP is verified, the account becomes usable, and the profile persists in the database.

B. Login:
The same user logs in again using the registered username or email and password. The account remains available after a page refresh and after a new browser session, subject to the configured session policy.

C. Password recovery:
The user requests a reset OTP. The backend generates and sends a fresh code. The old code becomes invalid after resending. A valid unexpired OTP enables password reset. The new password works and the old password no longer works.

D. Persistence:
The user creates a scan, bookmark, event, or triage decision. The record remains after refreshing the website and signing in again.

E. Security:
An unauthenticated user cannot access protected APIs. A regular user cannot invoke administrator-only operations. Invalid, expired, or reused OTPs fail. Passwords and OTPs never appear in plaintext storage or production logs.

F. Detection and response:
A synthetic security event is ingested through the real event API, normalized, assessed, stored, and displayed as an explicitly labelled test event. Its explanation, risk score, approval state, and audit record are consistent with the actual backend results.

G. Audit:
A new action appends a persistent audit record. The audit verification endpoint can detect an invalid chain. It must not report success after a detectable integrity failure.

H. Visual preservation:
The existing website remains recognisably the same application. Existing background, effects, charts, visuals, navigation, and working pages remain intact.

I. Integration honesty:
An unconfigured provider must show an unavailable or not-configured state. A local heuristic, synthetic event, simulated response, or fallback explanation must never be presented as the result of an unavailable real service.

